ntdll: a wait of the emulator does not take the wake-up of a 32-bit program's wait

dlls/ntdll/sync.c
- RtlWaitOnAddress() sleeps in NtWaitForAlertByThreadId(), which uses the one alert flag a
  thread has. In a 32-bit program two such waits can be under way on one thread: the
  program's (the 32-bit ntdll queues the thread and goes to make the system call) and, on
  the way there, the emulator's, which is 64-bit code on the same thread waiting for one of
  its own locks through the 64-bit ntdll. When the wake-ups for both arrive before the thread
  runs, they are one flag. The 64-bit wait took it and returned; the 32-bit wait then slept
  for good, its queue entry already removed by its waker, so no later wake-up found it.
  Seen in a trace: thread 0234 alerts 0240 twice in a row, for 0x150100082 (the emulator's
  lock) and for 00956DD2 (the program's), and 0240 never runs again. Portal 2 froze at start
  with every thread waiting; a Direct3D 9 test froze in 4 runs of 40.
- In a process with a 32-bit side, the 64-bit RtlWaitOnAddress() with no time limit now
  keeps waiting when it is alerted while still queued (the alert was for another wait), and
  sets the flag again when it leaves, for the wait it was inside. The outer wait may wake
  once for nothing, which callers of RtlWaitOnAddress() have to allow for anyway.
  After it: 60 runs of 60 finish.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
index f4090d5..c77960a 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
@@ -903,6 +903,26 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI RtlWaitOnAddress( const void *addr, const void *cmp, SIZE_T size
 
     spin_unlock( &queue->lock );
 
+#ifdef _WIN64
+    /* FineWine: a thread has one alert flag, and in a 32-bit program two waits can be using
+     * it at once. The program's own code (the 32-bit ntdll) puts itself on a queue and goes
+     * to wait; on the way the emulator, which is 64-bit code on the same thread, has to wait
+     * for one of its own locks, here. Two wake-ups, one for each wait, are one flag: this
+     * wait took it, the program's wait then slept for good with its queue entry already
+     * removed, and the program froze (Portal 2 at start, one test run in about fifteen;
+     * 2026-11-21). So in a 32-bit program an alert that is not for this wait is not taken as
+     * its wake-up, and the flag is set again on the way out for the wait this one was inside. */
+    if (NtCurrentTeb()->WowTebOffset && !timeout)
+    {
+        for (;;)
+        {
+            ret = NtWaitForAlertByThreadId( NULL, NULL );
+            if (ret != STATUS_ALERTED || !ReadPointerAcquire( (void * volatile *)&entry.addr )) break;
+        }
+        if (ret == STATUS_ALERTED) NtAlertThreadByThreadId( (HANDLE)(ULONG_PTR)entry.tid );
+    }
+    else
+#endif
     ret = NtWaitForAlertByThreadId( NULL, timeout );
 
     /* We may have already been removed by a call to RtlWakeAddressSingle() or RtlWakeAddressAll(). */
